<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.2: Linear Function Constant Rate of Change</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01517</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.2: Linear Function Constant Rate of Change</md:title>
    <md:uuid>49621e04-9a18-49b3-9f76-cc14e354998d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph of <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> where <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>5</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">

        <media alt="Line graph showing a straight line with positive slope passing through points (1,5), (2,7), (3,9), (4,11), and (5,13) on an x-y grid, with labeled axes x (horizontal) for 5 and 10 with grid lines at the unit, and y (vertical) at 5, 10 and 15 with grid lines for each unit.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_17_03_01.svg"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">1. How do the values of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> change whenever <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> increases by 1, for
            instance, when it increases from 1 to 2, or from 19 to 20? Be prepared to explain or
            show how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00006">The value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> increases by 2 whenever
            the input increases by 1. For example:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">a. From the graph, we can see the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-value increasing by
              2 when the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-value increases by
              1.</para>
            <para id="para-00013">b. <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. The difference
              between <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is
              2. Likewise, <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>20</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>45</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>19</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>43</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>20</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>19</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">2. Here is an expression we can use to find the difference in the
            values of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> when the input changes
            from <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00008">
            <m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>[</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>]</m:mi>
                  <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                  <m:mi>[</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>]</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00017">Does this expression have the same value as what you found in the
            previous questions? Show your reasoning.
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00011">Yes. <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Replacing <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> with <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and performing arithmetic
            gives <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">3. How do the values of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> change whenever <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> increases by 4? Explain
            or show how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00015"> The values of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> change by 8 if the input
            is increased by 4. Increasing the input by 4 is the same as increasing the input by 1
            four successive times, and each time the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> increased by 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">4. Write an expression that shows the change in the values of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> when the input value
            changes from <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00019">
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> can be
            expressed as <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>[</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>]</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mi>[</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>]</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">5. Show or explain how that expression has a value of 8.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00023">Applying the distributive property to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>[</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>]</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mi>[</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>]</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> gives us: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, which equals 8.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00004"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_15_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Determining Rate of Change of Linear Functions</title>
        <para id="para-00027">For the table below, assume the function <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is defined for all real
          numbers. Calculate <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>△</m:mi>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> in the last
          column. (The symbol <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>△</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> in this context means “change
          in.”) What do you notice about <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>△</m:mi>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>? Could the function be linear
          or exponential? Write a linear or an exponential function formula that generates the same
          input–output pairs as given in the table.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="3">
<<<<<<< HEAD
            <tbody>
=======
          
  <tbody>
>>>>>>> a9b3d22748ccf4d962d8f71574c293c2a589b83c
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>△</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> -3 </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
                <entry> 5 </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 4 </entry>
                <entry> 9 </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 5 </entry>
                <entry> 13 </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00028">For each row, you subtract <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> values. The
          first row would be <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, then <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, then <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, and then <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>13</m:mn>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. Think back to the lessons on
          the slope or rate of change of a linear function. The numerator was always the change in <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> or output, which is the same as
          the change in <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. Notice that
          all the changes in consecutive terms are the same, 4. This means that there is a constant
          rate of change and this particular function is linear.</para>
        <para id="para-00029">Since this function is linear, an equation will have the form <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mi>e</m:mi>
              <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
              <m:mtext> </m:mtext>
              <m:mi>o</m:mi>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
              <m:mtext> </m:mtext>
              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              <m:mi>g</m:mi>
              <m:mi>e</m:mi>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mi>i</m:mi>
              <m:mi>n</m:mi>
